<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the notarized macOS build.

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime (codesign's "options runtime"
  flag), and the hardened runtime denies JIT by default. atomscan needs JIT:
  yara-x compiles its rules to WebAssembly and runs them through
  wasmtime/cranelift, which maps executable pages at load time. Without
  allow-jit, a hardened-runtime build dies the first time a ruleset loads,
  i.e. on anything that actually scans.

  allow-jit alone turned out not to be enough, so the escalation this file used
  to describe as hypothetical has happened. The v2.5.0 release binaries are
  correctly signed and notarized (spctl reports "Notarized Developer ID"), and
  the version and help flags both run, yet any actual scan is killed in about a
  second:

      signal:      SIGKILL (Code Signature Invalid)
      termination: CODESIGNING / Invalid Page
      frame:       cleave::yara_engine::YaraEngine::scan_bytes_with_inline

  Re-signing that same binary with nothing changed but the entitlements is what
  isolates it: allow-jit alone reproduces the SIGKILL, allow-jit plus
  allow-unsigned-executable-memory scans cleanly. So cranelift is mapping
  executable pages that MAP_JIT plus pthread_jit_write_protect_np does not
  cover, and allow-jit cannot authorize them on its own.

  This is the weaker posture the old comment warned about, and it is the reason
  the smoke test in build.yml now runs a real scan: the version and help flags
  never compile a ruleset, so they never touch the JIT, and they passed the
  whole time this was broken.

  Keep this comment free of doubled hyphens. They are illegal inside an XML
  comment, and codesign's AMFI parser rejects the whole file ("Failed to parse
  entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error") even though plutil accepts it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
